I write maintainable and adaptable programs by separating responsibilities into components instead of placing all of the functionality into one large program. 
The CRUD Python module from Project One was an example of this approach. Rather than writing MongoDB connection and query logic directly throughout the dashboard, 
I created an AnimalShelter class that handled the database operations. In Project Two, I was then able to reuse that module as the interface between MongoDB and the dashboard. 
One of the main advantages of this approach was that the database logic and user interface remained separate. Changes to the dashboard did not require rewriting the underlying database functionality. 
It also made debugging easier because I could determine whether a problem originated in the database connection, CRUD module, or dashboard code. Readable naming, comments, 
and functions with specific responsibilities also made the application easier to understand and modify.

The CRUD module could be reused in other applications that need access to the same database. For example, a different web application, reporting tool, 
or administrative interface could use the same class without recreating the MongoDB connection and CRUD operations. The module could also be expanded with additional query methods or
adapted to work with other MongoDB collections. I approach a problem by first identifying the required result and then breaking the larger problem into smaller components 
that can be implemented and tested individually. For the Grazioso Salvare project, this meant separating the problem into the database, CRUD interface, filtering requirements, 
interactive table, and visualizations. I could verify each component before combining them into the complete dashboard.

This project differed from many previous programming assignments because the objective was not simply to make an individual algorithm or function produce the correct output. 
Multiple technologies had to communicate with each other to satisfy a client's requirements. The MongoDB database had to provide the correct records, the Python module had to retrieve them, 
and the Dash callbacks had to translate user interaction into database queries and visual changes. For future database projects, I would use a similar incremental approach. 
I would begin by analyzing the client's data and requirements, determine an appropriate database structure, establish and test the database interface, 
and then build application functionality on top of that foundation. I would also consider factors such as indexing, authentication, validation, maintainability, 
and how the data will actually be queried before finalizing the database design.

Computer scientists use computational tools and systematic problem-solving techniques to turn requirements and data into useful systems. Writing code is part of that process, 
but the larger responsibility involves understanding a problem, designing an appropriate solution, testing that solution, and making information useful to the people who need it. The Grazioso Salvare 
dashboard demonstrates why this matters. The organization could manually search thousands of animal shelter records, but that would be inefficient and make it easier to 
overlook suitable rescue candidates. The application instead allows users to select a rescue category and automatically filter the database according to characteristics such as breed, age, and sex. 
The interactive table and visualizations then make the resulting information easier to examine. For an organization like Grazioso Salvare, software like this can reduce the amount of time spent manually 
analyzing data and allow employees to focus more of their attention on their actual mission. That is one of the ways computer science creates value: it transforms raw information and repetitive processes 
into systems that help people make decisions and accomplish their work more effectively.
